Subject: Thinking about thinking

*
Here's an additional perspective to this thread. In addition to all the
reading we do, it's also useful to target successful (you pick your own
definition) people, seek them out for an "informational" interview, and
chat with them about major challenges they've faced, how the challenges
were met, the lessons learned, and any changes in perspective gained.

Perhaps this is idiosyncratic, but I tend to gain much benefit from
listening to stories from real-live people who've made progress, and then
learning their perspectives on how/why. This has regularly helped me
improve the questions I ask in my own field, and enhanced my understanding
of the dynamics of related fields.

When I think about the smartest people I know personally (e.g., Trang Vo,
Eric Drexler, Tim May, Joe Woelfel, Tom Mwanika, Tom Muth, Pat Tobin,
Camille Marder, Mike Miller, ... ) the only commonality I see is that they
tend to ask really great -- and often simple -- questions. They differ
widely in reading levels, reading topics, recreation, politics, family,
culture, diet/chemical intake, theories of living, ... . But they do ask --
and then try to answer -- superb questions.

