Date: Wed, 29 Dec 1993 14:34:24 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Longevity:  Imprison us for living.

Tim May: 
> My biggest fear about government regulating life extension work and
> cryonics is that government will outlaw competitors to its National
> Health Care System....
> Once this happens, I can't imagine cryonics being allowed. Hospitals
> will handle most deaths, and will likely not release the bodies until
> "suitable steps" have been taken--several days in the morgue, autopsy,
> etc.

Cryonics already has to fight legal battles for hosptial access and
to prevent government autopsy, so this is nothing new.   Cryonics 
doesn't use hospital facilities now anyway; they do require access in 
cases where the patient dies in the hospital.  Clinton's health plan 
doesn't nationalize hospitals (only insurers), and cryonics isn't covered
by health insurance anyway, so neither of these are likely to be
severely effected.

> I know Duncan and Sandy like to tell us "we've already won," and in
> some ways this is true. But in many other ways, "they've already won."

This is a good point.  We win some, we lose some.  We have more 
transparent borders, but at the same time we have Waco, civil
forfeiture, the Brady Bill, ad nauseum.  Meanwhile, a few political 
activists sacrifice their own welfare for the libertarian  cause and 
the free riders tag along, scooping up most of the benefits.  From
a purely individualist perspective, the only rational choice is to
be a free rider.  Furthermore, many of these issues are far too complex 
for amateur political activists to understand, and they end up shooting 
themselves in the foot, acheiving an outcome very different from that
intended.

That's why I think it's more important to fight the small battles where we 
as a small web of trust benefit directly -- for example, getting more health 
care workers sympathetic to cryonics; for example, creating our own small 
crypto-businesses and on-line anarchist communities -- than to 
idealistically tilt at windmills by complaining about or trying to fight 
national policies on major issues with our little group.

