The redoubtable T. C. May writes:
>I most certainly don't think I need to "work
>things out" with people I disagree with.

That depends on how firmly attached one is to the person one disagrees
with.  I have a wife and child, and I don't want to alienate them, so I
have to "work things out".  The same goes for the people I work with, to a
lesser extent.  Tim, you're an independently wealthy bachelor, so you have
an ability greater than most people do to simply not associate with those
you disagree with.  This is a luxury I used to enjoy, but now I'm very
happy with this "husband and father" thing.

I agree that there is no point in working things out if the relationship is
easier to give up than to fix.  One of the annoying elements of the
wimp-symp meme-set is the sense that conflict is bad and must be smoothed
out as a matter of moral urgency.

My wife and I are associated with a cohousing group, which is an
organization of people who intend to build a subdivision and live in it,
and have group dinners and watch each other's kids and grow old in one
place.  It's an attempt to recapture some of the benefits of community that
have been lost in modern America.  The group makes decisions by concensus,
which I thought completely impractical when I first heard of it, but which
actually works amazingly well.  What you need is a group that's small
enough (25 families) and around long enough (3 years) for everybody to know
everybody else.  You also need lots of "working it out" and "process" and
visualization excercises and saying "Thank you for sharing those feelings".
 All the paraphenalia of 1980s interpersonal feelgoodology.  That kind of
stuff used to drive me nuts, and it still does when somebody tries to foist
it on me outside of the cohousing group.  But it turns out that if you're
trying to build and maintain a group that people won't abandon, that kind
of stuff is necessary and highly effective.

>
>This appears to be another one of those male-female things.

Probably because females in this culture are socialized to be good at
skills that maintain family integrity, like "working it out" and cookery
and loving babies.  Men are socialized to be good at these things too, but
not nearly to the extent women are.


