From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: MORALS: Mere Asthetics

Amara Graps:
> ... extended families with cousins,
> grandparents etc were more the norm back then.

Extended families tended to be the norm in the upper classes of
agricultural society, where enough grandparents lived 
for long enough to have a major impact, but most of the evolution 
that shaped our emotional insticts took place in hunter-gatherer 
societies.  Family structure in these have varied considerably.  
Couple-based families dominate in most hunter-gatherer societies, 
although some argument can be made that the entire communal 
tribe of closely related people constitutes a "family".  
In societies with uncertain paternity, there is a tendency for children 
to be cared for by the mother and mother's relatives (not the father or 
father's relatives).  There are some rarer forms, but the 
couple/tribe and mother's kin/tribe make up the vast majority
of cases.  Most children in prehistoric societies had no surviving 
grandparents.

